<h2 id="game">What Happened Saturday</h2>
<p>Watson started Cleveland's preseason opener against the Bears and played the first half, leading the Browns to a 10-0 lead before fumbling on a sack in the second quarter. He finished with two scoring drives against a mix of Chicago starters and backups, describing the outing afterward as a chance to "feel the hits and get tackled a little bit" for the first time in nearly two years. Shedeur Sanders, who took over for the second half against mostly Bears reserves, was intercepted by cornerback Beanie Bishop Jr. and didn't lead Cleveland to any points. Head coach Todd Monken has said the plan is to flip the script in Week 2 against Buffalo, giving Sanders the start and a similar workload.</p>
<h2 id="injury">Two Achilles Tears in Two Years</h2>
<p>The box score matters less than the fact that Watson played at all. He suffered a season-ending shoulder injury in 2023, returned in 2024, and then ruptured his Achilles tendon — twice — ending his 2024 season and keeping him off an NFL field until this preseason opener. Back-to-back Achilles tears in the same player is exceedingly rare at any level of football, let alone at quarterback, where lower-body push-off power and pocket mobility are central to the position even for a passer not known primarily for scrambling.</p>
