<h2 id="recovery">What the Recovery Timeline Says</h2>
<p>Most single Achilles tears carry a 9-to-12-month return-to-play window in the NFL. A second tear on the same or opposite leg typically resets that clock and adds caution to every stage of testing, since the surgical site and surrounding tissue haven't had a full uninterrupted rebuilding cycle. Teams generally use a layered protocol before clearing a player for live contact — strength symmetry between limbs, jump testing, sprint mechanics, and finally change-of-direction drills — regardless of position.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>For the Full Testing Breakdown</strong>
  We covered exactly what this ladder looks like, using Daniel Jones's single Achilles recovery as the case study, in our piece on <a class="inline" href="/nfl-acl-achilles-comeback-athletic-benchmarks">the athletic benchmarks behind every NFL ACL and Achilles comeback</a>. Watson's situation — two tears rather than one — puts him at the far end of that same testing spectrum.
</div>
