<h2 id="battle">The Browns QB Battle</h2>
<p>Cleveland has treated the quarterback job as a genuine open competition throughout camp, with Watson and Sanders alternating first-team reps in practice before Saturday's split-squad approach in the opener. Watson's edge in Week 1 came against a mix of Bears starters and backups, while Sanders faced mostly reserves — a wrinkle worth remembering before reading too much into either performance. The real test comes in Week 2, when the roles flip against Buffalo and each quarterback sees a different level of competition than he did in Chicago.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How rare is it for an NFL player to tear the same or opposite Achilles twice?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Extremely rare, particularly at quarterback. Most NFL Achilles recoveries involve a single tear with a 9-to-12-month return window. A second tear resets much of that recovery process and typically extends caution around clearance for live contact.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Who won the Browns' preseason QB battle in Week 1?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Deshaun Watson was considered the more effective of the two quarterbacks in Cleveland's preseason opener against the Bears, leading two scoring drives. Shedeur Sanders, who played mostly against Chicago's backups, threw an interception and didn't lead a scoring drive. The competition remains open heading into Week 2.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">When did Deshaun Watson last play in an NFL game before this preseason?</div>
<div class="faq-a">October 20, 2024, when he suffered the second of his two Achilles tears. Saturday's preseason opener against the Bears was his first game action since.</div>
